<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Bws.Core\Bws.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    WPF UI, MIT, the base of this window's appearance since 2026-08-02. ADR-24 and ADR-15
    carry the decision, docs/10-WPF-UI.md carries what it costs and the five traps that are
    not in their documentation.

    Version pinned rather than floating. A control library that changes what the product looks
    like because a restore picked up a newer build is a change nobody decided.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="WPF-UI" Version="4.3.0" />

    <!--
      CsWin32, for one call: telling the window manager that the title bar above this window is a
      dark one. Merging the library's dark dictionaries styles everything INSIDE the window and
      says nothing about the bar, so Windows drew its own in #4C4A48 against content at #202020 -
      measured on screen 2026-08-10, backlog 148.
      A build time generator rather than a new dependency for the product, which is the same
      standing this package already has in Bws.Core - `ADR-2` and `ADR-15` say so in as many
      words, and PrivateAssets keeps it out of what ships.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32" Version="0.3.298">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Per-monitor DPI awareness, ADR-25. WPF is system DPI aware by default, which means Windows
    stretches the window bitmap on any monitor whose scaling differs from the one the session
    logged on with - and stretched text is blurry in the literal sense. Measured before this
    line existed: the process reported SYSTEM_AWARE.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <!-- CsWin32 emits a pointer signature for DwmSetWindowAttribute, backlog 148. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ONE FILE, AND UNTIL 2026-09-09 PublishSingleFile PRODUCED SIX.

    MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED ABOUT, by the pre-release audit of that day: publishing this
    project self-contained and single-file left Bws.Gui.exe beside D3DCompiler_47_cor3.dll,
    wpfgfx_cor3.dll, PresentationNative_cor3.dll, PenImc_cor3.dll and vcruntime140_cor3.dll -
    about eight megabytes of WPF's own native halves. The single-file bundler leaves native
    libraries out by default because they have to be loaded from a path on disk, and nothing in
    the build said so, so the promise and the output had disagreed since the day the window
    existed.

    WHY THAT IS A PRODUCT PROBLEM AND NOT A PACKAGING PREFERENCE. Phase 1 of the specification
    promises a portable executable, and PreferencesFile describes the person it is for as "the
    admin who copies the executable to the twentieth server". Copying Bws.Gui.exe alone is
    exactly what that person does.

    WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN REASONED ABOUT - AND THE PREDICTION WAS WRONG. The
    runtime extracts the native halves into the temporary directory on the first launch of a
    version and reuses them afterwards, 7.9 MB of them, so the expectation written down before
    the measurement was that a first run would be well over the budget. From launch to a row on
    the screen, on this machine, published self-contained, tools/gui-probe/first-row.ps1:

      six files, nothing to extract      798 - 960 ms   5 passes, a sixth discarded as cold
      one file, already extracted        776 - 978 ms   5 passes, a sixth discarded as cold
      one file, first run of a version   829 - 982 ms   5 cycles, extraction deleted each time

    THE SPREAD IS WIDER THAN ANY DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE THREE, so what this says is that the
    change costs nothing measurable here - including on the run that does the extracting, which
    is the one it was expected to cost. All three stay inside the one second section 8.1 allows.

    The file grows by what it now carries: 163 071 236 bytes to 171 289 067.

    The command line project deliberately does NOT have this. It has no native libraries of its
    own, so its publish already produced one file, and a property that changes nothing is a
    property somebody later has to work out the purpose of.

    WHAT IS NOT SOLVED HERE, said rather than left to be found: a publish still writes two .pdb
    files beside the executable. They are debugging symbols, nothing needs them to run, and
    whether a release ships them is a packaging question rather than this one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      WithCulture=false is load-bearing here for the same reason it is in Bws.Cli: MSBuild
      reads the ".en." in the name as a culture and compiles the file into a satellite
      assembly instead of embedding it, stripping the culture out of the resource name on the
      way. The build stays green and the window comes up showing keys instead of words.

      These are plain data we load and choose between ourselves, not .NET satellite
      resources. Every gui.<language>.json added later needs the same two attributes.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Resources\gui.en.json"
                      LogicalName="Bws.Gui.Resources.gui.en.json"
                      WithCulture="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Windows shows this as the file description, in properties and in Task Manager. -->
    <AssemblyTitle>Better Windows Services</AssemblyTitle>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ONE ICON FILE FOR BOTH SHIPPED PROJECTS, which is why it sits above them rather than in
    either. Two copies of an icon is two icons that will differ, and nothing would report it.
    The same trade tests/SourceTree.cs already makes one directory down.

    THIS ALSO SETS THE WINDOW'S ICON AND THAT IS NOT AN ASSUMPTION. A WPF window with no
    Window.Icon of its own falls back to the assembly icon, which covers the title bar, the
    taskbar button and the Alt+Tab list. Documented behaviour, not a side effect:
    https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.windows.window.icon
    So there is deliberately no Icon= in MainWindow.xaml - a second declaration would be a
    second thing to keep in step.

    Drawn by tools/icon/bean.ps1 and packed by tools/icon/make-ico.ps1, both outside git.
    Ten sizes: 16, 20, 24, 32, 36, 40, 48 as DIBs and 64, 96, 256 as PNG. 36 is in nobody's
    published minimum and is there because at the 150 per cent scaling this was measured on,
    36 is exactly what the taskbar asks for. docs/PROJEKT-IKONY-20260907.md carries the rest.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\bws.ico</ApplicationIcon>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>